{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an in-game purchase being completed without tapping a purchase control and without the device's required fingerprint or password confirmation. This is an unintended purchase caused by the absence or bypass of a confirmation step, matching Accidental Purchases. The mention of a possible glitch is uncertain and does not erase the explicitly described purchase behavior; no discount or special pricing is stated for the starter pack, so Never-Ending Lure is not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "I didn't tap anything and my settings are set up to where I have to input my fingerprint or password to make a purchase and it did not do that.",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases",
      "rationale": "The purchase allegedly completed without the player's intended tap and without the required confirmation or authentication step."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a merge game whose board and inventory have become overcrowded. The later archiving system does not remove the storage burden because archived items still consume inventory space, leaving deletion as the apparent way to cope. That is realized inventory pressure and forfeiture of held items, which supports Waste Aversion. The mention of insufficient energy does not state a regeneration timer or other wait schedule, so it does not support Playing by Appointment. The ad complaint concerns external advertising and is out of scope.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "they finally started \"archiving\" but expect you to use your inventory space for them??? Hello?!? DELETE.",
      "rule_applied": "Capped inventory forces you to destroy items or upgrade inventory",
      "rationale": "Archived items still occupy limited inventory space, forcing the player toward deleting items and forfeiting their invested value."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that appears after every over, making the player view ads at a recurring gameplay event. This is a forced ad mechanic and satisfies Advertisement. The game closing when the player accidentally taps the ad, requiring an app restart, is attributed to bad app behavior or a technical fault, so it does not independently support a dark-pattern label. The review does not clearly describe a deliberately misleading, hidden, or undersized cancel control, so UI Misdirection is not assigned. No external search is needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "After every over, an ad pops up.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "An advertisement is imposed at a fixed in-game event, compelling the player to encounter it during play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes increased interface friction from repeated pop-up screens. The player must dismiss an \"are you sure you want to quit\" screen with an X before continuing, which obstructs the normal opt-out or continuation path and fits UI Misdirection. The weekly challenge score pop-ups are merely described as numerous and annoying; no reward, schedule pressure, competition, or advertising mechanic is clearly established. The increased cost is a pricing complaint without a described purchase mechanism, so it does not support a monetary label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "are you sure you want to quit screens that you have to X out of before continuing",
      "rule_applied": "vs Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The game forces the player to dismiss an intervening screen before continuing, obstructing the normal continuation or exit flow."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes in-game advertisements and pop-ups appearing repeatedly before gameplay, with one following another and delaying access to play. This is an in-game ad burden that effectively compels the player to get through the ads before playing. The review does not establish that the pop-ups are purchase prompts, use misleading controls, or impose a timed appointment mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the seemingly endless in-game ads and pop-ups that cascade one after another so much that by the time I can actually play",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player describes repeated in-game advertisements that must be endured before gameplay can begin."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes advertisements appearing inside the game despite the claim that it was ad free. It also describes an obstructed ad exit: leaving the advertisement requires opening the Play Store, which adds friction and data use. The external hype about the game being ad free is marketing material and is out of scope, but the in-game ads and their difficult dismissal are in scope.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "Although the ads are seldom, so far, they are there all the same.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly reports encountering advertisements within the game."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "to get out of the ad, you have to go into the play store",
      "rule_applied": "vs Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The ad obstructs the normal opt-out or exit path by requiring the player to enter the Play Store to leave it."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a streak mechanic that rewards continued daily play and resets progress to the beginning when the game records a missed day. Although the reset appears to have been triggered incorrectly because the reviewer says they did not miss the day, the underlying deliberate rule is still a daily streak penalty. The text does not describe a time gate blocking broader progression, so Playing by Appointment does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "said I broke my streak and though I did not, it sent me right back to the beginning",
      "rule_applied": "definition; R11 bug-attribution rule",
      "rationale": "A streak that resets to the beginning when the game says a day was missed is a daily-return incentive with a penalty, even though the reviewer says the reset was erroneous."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a competitive match in Duel/Event modes, with ranked points at stake. The lag, unresponsive controls, network warning, and sudden stoppage are attributed to a technical malfunction, so those faults themselves do not receive a dark-pattern label. However, the game applies an explicit match-loss penalty after the malfunction: it declares the player defeated and removes roughly 30–50 ranked points. Under R11, a malfunction does not suppress Mandatory Marathon when the underlying game rule forfeits match progress upon an interrupted session. The review does not describe advertising, payment, rewards, currencies, or a chance-based outcome.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "Duel Mode",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Duel Mode describes player-versus-player competition, and the associated ranked progress confirms that competitive standing is part of the mechanic."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_MandatoryMarathon",
      "span": "game has suddenly stopped and says YOU HAVE LOST and then nearly -30 to -50 gaming points are lessed from my Ranked game progress",
      "rule_applied": "vs Loss caused by malfunction",
      "rationale": "Although the stoppage is attributed to lag or a technical fault, the game treats the interrupted match as a loss and removes ranked progress, imposing a deliberate forfeiture penalty when the session ends unexpectedly."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes three mechanics or behaviors. The loading delay is a technical/service delay, not an obscured download requirement, so it does not support Tech_FragmentedDownloads or a temporal label. The reviewer says a bonus feature called the \"bonus lady\" rarely appears or matches, which implies an infrequent, chance-based reward delivery system and supports Reward Mania. The reviewer also explicitly says they are buying coins, which describes acquiring in-game currency with real money and supports Intermediate Currency. The term \"ww4\" is unclear, so I searched it, but the search did not establish a specific Cashman Casino feature; no label is based on that term alone.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the bonus lady rarely comes out or even matches with anything on ww4",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards",
      "rationale": "The bonus feature appears infrequently and depends on chance-based matching, so the delivery mechanism is unpredictable rather than fixed."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "buying coins from them",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes purchasing virtual coins with real money, which is the conversion of real-world money into in-game currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Cashman Casino Slots Games \"bonus lady\" WW4",
  "search_result": "The search surfaced general slot references and a Cashman Casino jackpot reference, but did not establish what \"WW4\" or \"bonus lady\" specifically refers to. ([liquidandgrit.com](https://www.liquidandgrit.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/Slots-Design-Report-April-2019.pdf?utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several complaints but only two clearly named mechanics. The cheating statement attributes misconduct to other players, not to a game design that rewards harmful behavior, so it does not support S_EncouragesAntiSocialBehavior. Peg-E and the race car game are named events, but the review does not describe their rules, rewards, timers, or competition structure. Gold duplicates describe repeated unwanted collectible rewards; the search establishes that these are duplicate stickers in MONOPOLY GO!'s sticker-trading system, which supports Reward Mania. The complaint about not having enough trades per day describes a player-to-player item exchange mechanic, supporting Reciprocity. The shortage of dice and the 500-space statement do not by themselves identify a coded dark pattern.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Way to many gold duplicates!",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards; duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomised pool",
      "rationale": "The reviewer reports repeatedly receiving duplicate gold collectible items, an unwanted duplicate-reward outcome in the game's sticker reward system."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Not enough trades a day!",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Feeling obligated to return a favor, share resources, trade an item, etc.",
      "rationale": "The span identifies a player-to-player trading system with a daily exchange limit; official game information confirms that duplicate stickers are traded with friends and that trading is capped. "
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "MONOPOLY GO gold duplicates sticker trading limit Peg-E race car game",
  "search_result": "Official MONOPOLY GO sources establish that duplicate stickers are copies that can be traded with friends and that sticker trading has daily limits; gold-sticker trading can also be restricted to special events. ([monopolygo.helpshift.com](https://monopolygo.helpshift.com/hc/en/3-monopoly-go/faq/276-duplicate-stickers/?pubDate=20250530&utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a competitive dice-based game in which outcomes are allegedly predetermined or manipulated against them: regardless of skill, they say they will lose, while opponents receive favorable counts and they receive unfavorable ones. That is a claim that the game determines outcomes rather than letting play skill and chance decide them, which supports Illusion of Control. The mention of opponents also explicitly describes player-versus-player competition. The references to guilt, time wasting, and glitches are evaluations or technical complaints and do not independently support a label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "if you are meant to lose, no matter how good you play you will definitley lose and in most of the cases this is the scene always..the opponents get good counts and you get small counts",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the game fixes or biases the outcome, giving opponents favorable counts and the player unfavorable counts regardless of skill."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the opponents get good counts and you get small counts",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the player competing against opponents in the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The external advertisement is described as misleading, but advertising placed outside the installed game is out of scope under R13, so it does not receive a label. Within the game, the reviewer describes repeatedly playing a Candy Crush-like level to earn stars, with stars required to make progress such as walking Austin. This is a repetitive task used for progression, which supports Grinding. The loss of points after failing a level does not by itself show a retention or stopping mechanic covered by another label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Always you play one game you just get one star only. And all your points get lost if you don't make that level. Even for walking Austin you will have to earn a star by playing stupid game.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes repeatedly playing the same type of level to earn stars needed for game progress, which is a tedious task cycle tied to advancement."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that cannot be dismissed, blocking continued play until the player closes and restarts the game. This makes the advertisement compulsory and also shows that the interface lacks a usable opt-out control. The need to restart is a technical workaround, not a deliberate session rule involving unsaved progress, so it does not support Mandatory Marathon.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "Can't close the ads",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player is compelled to remain in an in-game advertisement because it cannot be closed before gameplay continues."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Can't close the ads",
      "rule_applied": "vs Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The advertisement obstructs the opt-out by providing no usable way to close it."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes rewarded advertisements: the player watches ads in expectation of receiving in-game rewards, but a connection failure prevents those rewards from being granted. The failed connection is a technical problem, but the underlying rewarded-ad mechanic is still explicitly present and is codable. The generic mention of microtransactions does not identify a progress gate, currency conversion, purchase prompt, or other specific monetary mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "when you watch the ads, it conveniently \"can't connect\" so you can't get rewards for the ads you watched",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes incentivized ad viewing, where watching advertisements is tied to receiving rewards; the connection failure affects delivery of the reward but does not remove the underlying ad mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an idle base-building game in which progress involves leveling and waiting for building timers to finish. The waiting is an in-game timer that delays advancement, matching the Wait to Play indicator. Heroes, scouting bases, and gathering resources are named as game features, but the review does not explicitly describe competition, repetitive grinding, or a reward schedule that is currently present. The request for more mini games or periodic rewards is hypothetical and is not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "leveling & waiting for building times",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "Building times impose in-game timers that make the player wait before progressing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a mismatch between the stated 1GB size and the game's actual storage demand: despite the game being presented as 1GB, it fills the phone's storage. This implies that the true storage requirement is larger than the apparent advertised size, which matches Fragmented Downloads. The comments about the phone model and graphics are not dark-pattern mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "Tech_FragmentedDownloads",
      "span": "why you give the screen size 1GB my phone is storage full for this game",
      "rule_applied": "Small advertised App Store size vs. large mandatory in-game download",
      "rationale": "The reviewer contrasts the stated 1GB size with the game filling the phone's storage, implying that the actual storage requirement is obscured until installation or play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a resource-gated progression loop. Each level consumes credits, and the rising cost leaves the player unable to continue immediately, imposing an hour-long wait before play can resume. The game also offers a paid route: spending real cash to obtain credits and continue playing. Because credits are the in-game currency acquired with real money, the review also describes an intermediate-currency conversion. The mention that a level now costs 2.8k after the player had only earned that amount three levels earlier shows escalating costs, but it does not clearly describe stranded leftover currency or a concrete waste event, so Waste Aversion is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "which leaves you waiting an hour to play again",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "An in-game resource restriction imposes an hour-long timer before the player can resume playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "or shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Skip / Pay to Progress",
      "rationale": "The player can spend real money to obtain the credits needed to bypass the wait and continue progressing through levels."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency",
      "rationale": "The span describes real-world cash being exchanged for in-game credits used to play levels."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a 7-day streak that can be lost, which is a daily-return incentive with a penalty for losing accumulated progress. Although they say it happened for no reason and contacted Zynga about the issue, the underlying streak-reset mechanic is still explicitly present; the possible malfunction does not suppress the Daily Rewards label under R11. No other game mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I've had 3 separate times times to where I've lost my 7 day streak for no reason whatsoever",
      "rule_applied": "definition; R11 loss caused by malfunction",
      "rationale": "A 7-day streak that can be lost is a daily-return incentive with an explicit penalty for losing the streak, even though the reviewer attributes the losses to an unexplained problem."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two game mechanics. First, the game presents daily missions, but the reviewer says the available attempts are insufficient to complete them; this does not explicitly describe a login reward, streak, or penalty for missing a day, so T_DailyRewards is not assigned. Second, new chances to play arrive only after an in-game timer of nearly two minutes, which is a Wait to Play mechanic under Playing by Appointment. The complaint that items do not match orders describes an outcome problem but does not establish a coded dark-pattern mechanic such as rigging.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it takes nearly 2 minutes for new chances to play",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "The game imposes a timed wait before the player receives new chances to continue playing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a casino-slots app whose core play is gambling: the player is expected to spend money for a chance to win, but says winning is effectively impossible. The slot format also identifies a chance-based reward system that keeps play centered on repeated attempts and uncertain outcomes. The complaint does not describe a specific rigging mechanism, near-misses, progress gating, or a required wait, so those labels are not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "why would I ever come back and spend more money when it is impossible to when",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player describes spending money in a casino-slots game for the chance of winning a reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "make these things so fun to play but impossible to ever win",
      "rule_applied": "Gacha mechanics / variable rewards",
      "rationale": "In the context of Lightning Link Casino Slots, the span describes repeated play driven by uncertain chance-based winnings, the reward-delivery pattern covered by Reward Mania."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}
